WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:06.580 --> 00:00:11.600
Shall we then move rapidly to Simon for the
Chief Executive’s Report?

00:00:11.600 --> 00:00:12.600
Thank you, Malcolm.

00:00:12.600 --> 00:00:16.590
As you say, we have a lot on the agenda, so
I am going to be very concise.

00:00:16.590 --> 00:00:23.430
My report principally records the range of
external-facing activities which I and a number

00:00:23.430 --> 00:00:25.500
of colleagues have been engaged in since our
last meeting.

00:00:25.500 --> 00:00:30.789
I do just want to mention four things specifically.

00:00:30.789 --> 00:00:38.660
The first is to record my thanks and our thanks
to Ed and to Paul and to Karen and their teams

00:00:38.660 --> 00:00:42.720
for the successful production of our Annual
Report and Annual Accounts, which were laid

00:00:42.720 --> 00:00:46.550
before Parliament this week.

00:00:46.550 --> 00:00:53.620
I think the way in which they have been conducted,
the efficiency with which that process has

00:00:53.620 --> 00:00:59.210
worked this year, is a testament to not only
enormous effort but a great improvement in

00:00:59.210 --> 00:01:03.840
the processes that underpin our financial
reporting, so thanks particularly to Paul

00:01:03.840 --> 00:01:07.939
and Karen, and Ed as Chair of our Audit Committee.

00:01:07.939 --> 00:01:15.330
Secondly, to report that substantial work
is underway across NHS England.

00:01:15.330 --> 00:01:21.080
Paul, Barbara, others, together with their
opposite numbers Monitor and TDA in respect

00:01:21.080 --> 00:01:26.820
of the financial position of the National
Health Service this year, and in particular

00:01:26.820 --> 00:01:31.670
the efforts that we described at our last
Board meeting to ensure that CCGs are funding

00:01:31.670 --> 00:01:38.231
appropriate volumes of emergency and elective
activity in providers: that process has, I

00:01:38.231 --> 00:01:41.210
think, now worked its way through.

00:01:41.210 --> 00:01:46.960
In addition, a lot will hinge on the effective
execution of the cost take-outs on temporary

00:01:46.960 --> 00:01:53.479
staffing that providers are now engaged in,
Monitor and TDA setting a dial-back on the

00:01:53.479 --> 00:01:57.400
use of offering work contracts, the hourly
rates, the provider-specific totals.

00:01:57.400 --> 00:02:05.240
Frankly, that is going to be the single biggest
contribution to reducing the projected provider

00:02:05.240 --> 00:02:07.390
overspends this year.

00:02:07.390 --> 00:02:13.879
Alongside that there are also controls on
the use of management solvency spending, the

00:02:13.879 --> 00:02:19.810
extra commission volumes need to be reflected
in provider plans, and Monitor and TDA have

00:02:19.810 --> 00:02:26.860
signalled that they are in the process of
agreeing financial totals with each provider

00:02:26.860 --> 00:02:32.080
in the country to ensure that, overall, the
performance is substantially better than that

00:02:32.080 --> 00:02:35.070
which was being reported at the beginning
of the financial year.

00:02:35.070 --> 00:02:43.200
So most of the executional responsibilities
for that are sitting with Monitor and TDA,

00:02:43.200 --> 00:02:50.410
but very significant stakes for the whole
NHS in ensuring that that is delivered effectively.

00:02:50.410 --> 00:02:56.250
Thirdly, the Chancellor has announced that
the spending review will conclude on 25th

00:02:56.250 --> 00:02:57.250
November.

00:02:57.250 --> 00:03:01.990
So now we are gearing up, between ourselves,
the Department of Health and others, to ensure

00:03:01.990 --> 00:03:08.530
that as we think about the phasing of the
extra investment that the forward view requires,

00:03:08.530 --> 00:03:16.660
together with the refining of the components
that will drive the efficiency.

00:03:16.660 --> 00:03:20.020
The aim is that there will be substantial
engagement across the front line of the National

00:03:20.020 --> 00:03:25.400
Health Service now through early autumn, putting
flesh on the bones of what that efficiency

00:03:25.400 --> 00:03:30.290
deliverable will look like so that, at the
same time as the spending review investment

00:03:30.290 --> 00:03:35.050
totals are announced, we are also able to
set out clearly what the composition of the

00:03:35.050 --> 00:03:41.540
efficiency will be, its phasing and the who-does-what
piece, recognising that there are three parts

00:03:41.540 --> 00:03:42.540
of that.

00:03:42.540 --> 00:03:46.550
There will be things that individual organisations
across the NHS will need to do, there are

00:03:46.550 --> 00:03:53.011
things that the providers and commissioning
bodies collectively will need to do, and there

00:03:53.011 --> 00:04:00.510
are things that only nationally can be done,
either by the ALBs or the Department of Health.

00:04:00.510 --> 00:04:05.840
Then the fourth thing I would say is that
there are some important developments on our

00:04:05.840 --> 00:04:07.480
service redesign agenda.

00:04:07.480 --> 00:04:11.550
Bruce I think will talk about the important
work of the Independent Cancer Taskforce that

00:04:11.550 --> 00:04:13.040
has just been released.

00:04:13.040 --> 00:04:19.720
He has also been heavily engaged in the process
on shaping the seven-day services agenda.

00:04:19.720 --> 00:04:23.710
I also think it is worth recording, given
the public discussion that there has been

00:04:23.710 --> 00:04:30.039
on seven-day services, that as I say in my
paper, we need to distinguish the three separate

00:04:30.039 --> 00:04:33.559
problems that we are trying to solve here.

00:04:33.559 --> 00:04:39.210
Where this debate goes off track is when people
get these three issues mixed up.

00:04:39.210 --> 00:04:45.710
First up is the question of the excess mortality
for hospital inpatients on a weekend that

00:04:45.710 --> 00:04:50.180
Bruce and colleagues have documented and reaffirmed.

00:04:50.180 --> 00:04:55.849
Second is the question of ensuring that there
is readily accessible urgent and emergency

00:04:55.849 --> 00:05:00.909
care at evenings and weekends, as well as
during weekdays, out of hospital.

00:05:00.909 --> 00:05:06.310
Then third is ensuring that, to the greatest
extent possible, convenient routine out of

00:05:06.310 --> 00:05:10.029
hospital care, primary care, is available
for people who cannot get to it during the

00:05:10.029 --> 00:05:11.259
normal working week.

00:05:11.259 --> 00:05:14.999
So we have to think of it in those three buckets,
three different solution sets.

00:05:14.999 --> 00:05:20.300
The response to the first and the second in
part relates to the Urgent and Emergency Care

00:05:20.300 --> 00:05:22.840
Review, which we have previously discussed.

00:05:22.840 --> 00:05:27.550
So the final piece I wanted to mention is
that tomorrow we will be announcing the outcome

00:05:27.550 --> 00:05:32.279
of the Urgent and Emergency Care Vanguard
Process.

00:05:32.279 --> 00:05:36.309
Same sort of process as has been used for
the selection of the first 29.

00:05:36.309 --> 00:05:40.740
We will be having a paper later on about the
vanguard support process, but these eight

00:05:40.740 --> 00:05:45.629
parts of the country, which we will be announcing
tomorrow, are the people who are going to

00:05:45.629 --> 00:05:52.499
put their foot on the pedal in terms of fast-forwarding
the Urgent and Emergency Care Review implementation,

00:05:52.499 --> 00:05:57.810
so that we can actually see in practice how
that delivers us the efficiency and the patient

00:05:57.810 --> 00:06:03.509
quality and access benefits which are implicit
in Bruce and Keith’s review.

00:06:03.509 --> 00:06:07.800
Those were the four points I wanted to highlight,
Malcolm.

00:06:07.800 --> 00:06:08.800
Thank you very much.

00:06:08.800 --> 00:06:13.199
Can I invite any comments or questions to
the Chief Executive?

00:06:13.199 --> 00:06:14.659
No?

00:06:14.659 --> 00:06:15.189
Thank you very much.

